00:01:21.070 --> 00:01:22.820
So first of all, I
just wanted to give

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:26.410
a general high-level
overview of the GDPR.

00:01:26.410 --> 00:01:28.360
What is the GDPR?

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:33.940
The GDPR stands for the General
Data Protection Regulation

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:35.590
in Europe.

00:01:35.590 --> 00:01:40.600
And this is, by far, the most
significant data protection

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:45.170
legislation that came out from
Europe in the last 20 years.

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:47.950
And the last one, of course,
the European Data Protection

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:51.040
Directive 20 years ago--

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:54.580
and 20 years ago, it
was a very different era

00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:56.680
in terms of the internet.

00:01:56.680 --> 00:01:59.050
Internet was not
as commonly used,

00:01:59.050 --> 00:02:04.990
and especially, there was not
so much personal data on it.

00:02:04.990 --> 00:02:09.490
Some other big differences
between the European Data

00:02:09.490 --> 00:02:11.830
Protection Directive
from 20 years ago

00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:16.390
and the General Data
Protection Regulation

00:02:16.390 --> 00:02:20.470
is that the directive
is a directive, which

00:02:20.470 --> 00:02:23.530
means that it directed
member states to pass

00:02:23.530 --> 00:02:27.100
their own privacy legislation.

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:31.870
The result was similar laws
but somewhat of a patchwork,

00:02:31.870 --> 00:02:37.510
and that mades compliance very
challenging from customers

00:02:37.510 --> 00:02:40.440
and providers alike.

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:44.500
And the GDPR intends to
harmonize these laws,

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:46.900
and it's also a
regulation, which

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:50.950
means that it has a
direct effect on the law.

00:02:50.950 --> 00:02:53.830
So it's legally binding,
and member states

00:02:53.830 --> 00:02:59.350
don't have to pass
separate legislation.

00:02:59.350 --> 00:03:04.480
And of course, it
means to be much more

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:07.390
convenient for customers
and providers alike.

00:03:10.210 --> 00:03:14.980
Probably the most important
thing about the GDPR,

00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:19.120
that it enhances
data subject rights.

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:22.150
It's building on existing
data protection principles,

00:03:22.150 --> 00:03:28.010
such as data minimization,
storage limitation, purpose

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:28.810
limitation.

00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:30.260
What does it mean?

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:33.040
It means that when
a company collects

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:35.290
data for a specific
purpose, it should

00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:39.280
be processed for only that
purpose and only for a time

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:41.320
period necessary.

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:44.410
And of course, there are other
data protection principles,

00:03:44.410 --> 00:03:49.990
such as lawfulness, fairness,
transparency, data accuracy,

00:03:49.990 --> 00:03:52.300
integrity, and so forth.

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:55.090
We find all of these data
protection principles

00:03:55.090 --> 00:04:00.330
in the GDPR and the
data subject rights

00:04:00.330 --> 00:04:01.990
built on these principles.

00:04:01.990 --> 00:04:06.100
For example, data
integrity accuracy

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:10.040
means that data subject
rights have a right

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:13.510
to request to rectify
from companies,

00:04:13.510 --> 00:04:16.640
to store the data that--

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:18.970
from the companies
about the data

00:04:18.970 --> 00:04:21.220
that they store about them--

00:04:21.220 --> 00:04:25.330
so right to rectification,
right to access,

00:04:25.330 --> 00:04:27.850
right to restrict
for data procession,

00:04:27.850 --> 00:04:31.210
and also data deletion.

00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:35.380
The GDPR also introduces
two new rights.

00:04:35.380 --> 00:04:40.330
One is data portability, and the
other is right to [INAUDIBLE]..

00:04:40.330 --> 00:04:45.050
And I will talk about these
data subject rights later.

00:04:45.050 --> 00:04:49.380
And as part of the enhanced
data subject rights,

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:57.490
the GDPR also further expands
the international data transfer

00:04:57.490 --> 00:05:00.730
protection, which means
that if data flows outside

00:05:00.730 --> 00:05:03.430
of the European
Union, companies have

00:05:03.430 --> 00:05:07.390
to make sure that that data
has the adequate level of data

00:05:07.390 --> 00:05:08.980
protection.

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:12.820
And the GDPR also lists
additional mechanisms.

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:19.720
Compared to the previous
one, for example,

00:05:19.720 --> 00:05:21.270
where there are
contract clauses,

00:05:21.270 --> 00:05:24.010
now we also have
bilingual [INAUDIBLE]

00:05:24.010 --> 00:05:26.110
and other certification
mechanisms

00:05:26.110 --> 00:05:29.760
that companies can use.

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:33.020
All right, so what are
the five key things

00:05:33.020 --> 00:05:39.560
I wanted to talk about today in
terms of the regulation of GDPR

00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:41.524
and Google Cloud?

00:05:41.524 --> 00:05:47.620
Well, the first key difference
is that now the GDPR also

00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:50.720
applies to cloud providers.

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:54.680
It means that it's not
only the customer, you,

00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:59.090
but us, Google Cloud, are also
responsible for our compliance.

00:06:01.850 --> 00:06:06.020
Second, I also wanted to
talk about the sub-processor

00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:08.335
and what does it
mean in terms of--

00:06:08.335 --> 00:06:13.800
in our obligations for the GDPR,
that we work for sub-processors

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:18.099
to process customer data.

00:06:18.099 --> 00:06:19.640
I'm going to extend
a little bit more

00:06:19.640 --> 00:06:22.510
about the extended
data subject rights.

00:06:22.510 --> 00:06:25.310
I'm going to select
two rights, and I'm

00:06:25.310 --> 00:06:28.100
going to demonstrate
how we can support

00:06:28.100 --> 00:06:32.900
the customers [INAUDIBLE]
subject have requested

00:06:32.900 --> 00:06:35.430
them regarding their data.

00:06:35.430 --> 00:06:39.590
And fourth, I want to talk
about the incredible high fines

00:06:39.590 --> 00:06:42.680
that the GDPR
introduced, which can

00:06:42.680 --> 00:06:49.310
be as high as 20 million euros,
or 4% of the annual turnover.

00:06:49.310 --> 00:06:54.890
And finally, I wanted to
talk about GDPR and security,

00:06:54.890 --> 00:06:58.760
and I also wanted to
showcase some of the security

00:06:58.760 --> 00:07:00.890
product features
and configurations

00:07:00.890 --> 00:07:07.390
that we offer in G Suite that
can be helpful with compliance.

00:07:07.390 --> 00:07:09.700
So number one is
data processors.

00:07:09.700 --> 00:07:13.720
As I mentioned this
first point, now the GDPR

00:07:13.720 --> 00:07:17.960
also applies to cloud
providers, to us.

00:07:17.960 --> 00:07:18.950
So what does it mean?

00:07:18.950 --> 00:07:20.810
Maybe you have
heard the terms data

00:07:20.810 --> 00:07:23.420
controller and data processors.

00:07:23.420 --> 00:07:27.700
The GDPR reintroduces
these terms.

00:07:27.700 --> 00:07:30.890
They were already present in
the European Data Protection

00:07:30.890 --> 00:07:36.500
Directive, but now the GDPR
further qualifies and explains

00:07:36.500 --> 00:07:38.840
their meaning.

00:07:38.840 --> 00:07:43.010
Data controllers are
you, the customers

00:07:43.010 --> 00:07:48.140
who are primarily responsible
for data processing.

00:07:48.140 --> 00:07:49.100
What does it mean?

00:07:49.100 --> 00:07:53.300
That you are the one who
determines the purposes

00:07:53.300 --> 00:07:56.470
and means of data
processing, based

00:07:56.470 --> 00:07:58.250
on the wording of the law.

00:07:58.250 --> 00:08:02.450
In other words, it means
that the customer, the data

00:08:02.450 --> 00:08:06.080
controller, is the one
who decides on the why

00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:09.140
and how on the data processing.

00:08:09.140 --> 00:08:14.150
And we, as data processors, are
the ones who process data only

00:08:14.150 --> 00:08:19.670
based on your instructions and
on behalf of the controller.

00:08:22.890 --> 00:08:25.020
So let me tell you
more about this

00:08:25.020 --> 00:08:32.640
and how we try to make our
obligations as data processor.

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:35.909
But first of all, people
are very important.

00:08:35.909 --> 00:08:42.600
So we are really proud to
say that we have the world's

00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:45.330
foremost experts in
security, and they

00:08:45.330 --> 00:08:50.130
are responsible for information,
application, and network

00:08:50.130 --> 00:08:51.570
security as well.

00:08:51.570 --> 00:08:55.665
They are the ones who
maintain our defense system,

00:08:55.665 --> 00:08:58.170
build our security
infrastructure,

00:08:58.170 --> 00:09:03.250
decide on the security
review process, also helping

00:09:03.250 --> 00:09:05.840
with their review
process when we onboard

00:09:05.840 --> 00:09:08.580
vendors, sub-processors.

00:09:08.580 --> 00:09:12.660
And they are also the ones
who build Google's security

00:09:12.660 --> 00:09:17.190
infrastructure and
responsible for implementing

00:09:17.190 --> 00:09:18.315
Google's security policies.

00:09:21.020 --> 00:09:23.560
But we also have a
very extensive team

00:09:23.560 --> 00:09:27.440
of compliance
professionals and lawyers,

00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:32.110
including privacy lawyers,
and our responsibility

00:09:32.110 --> 00:09:35.170
as compliance
professionals is to assure

00:09:35.170 --> 00:09:38.740
that Google as a
whole, the company,

00:09:38.740 --> 00:09:42.220
are fully trained and
aware about the latest data

00:09:42.220 --> 00:09:44.440
protection standards.

00:09:44.440 --> 00:09:49.150
As an example, we design
Google's privacy training

00:09:49.150 --> 00:09:50.530
program.

00:09:50.530 --> 00:09:54.340
But we also have policy
and compliance experts

00:09:54.340 --> 00:09:59.350
who directly engage with
the regulators in Europe who

00:09:59.350 --> 00:10:04.930
are responsible for imposing
those really high fines.

00:10:04.930 --> 00:10:09.830
And whenever we're developing
features for compliance,

00:10:09.830 --> 00:10:12.460
we check with them
to make sure that we

00:10:12.460 --> 00:10:14.080
are going in the
right direction,

00:10:14.080 --> 00:10:19.960
and it can be helpful with our
customers' compliance needs.

00:10:19.960 --> 00:10:22.690
So it's fair to say
that we are really

00:10:22.690 --> 00:10:27.730
committed having customers
in their GDPR compliance.

00:10:27.730 --> 00:10:31.300
And we're not only saying this,
but we put our commitments

00:10:31.300 --> 00:10:33.190
to writing.

00:10:33.190 --> 00:10:38.890
As an example, we updated our
data processing agreements

00:10:38.890 --> 00:10:42.610
with new terms that reflect
the changes within the GDPR.

00:10:42.610 --> 00:10:45.400
And here, I wanted to
remind you that in case you

00:10:45.400 --> 00:10:52.120
haven't done so, please opt
in to the most recent data

00:10:52.120 --> 00:10:54.280
processing amendments.

00:10:54.280 --> 00:10:57.070
And I will show in
two slides later

00:10:57.070 --> 00:11:01.190
how you can do that if you
haven't done so by tuning

00:11:01.190 --> 00:11:05.380
into our GDPR Resource Center.

00:11:05.380 --> 00:11:10.690
We also state in our
data processing agreement

00:11:10.690 --> 00:11:14.210
how we, as data processors,
only process data

00:11:14.210 --> 00:11:18.000
based on your instructions.

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:22.270
Also, our employees are
required to sign confidentiality

00:11:22.270 --> 00:11:23.230
agreements.

00:11:23.230 --> 00:11:28.600
And they have to go through
mandatory privacy trainings.

00:11:28.600 --> 00:11:31.570
This is what we, the
privacy professional,

00:11:31.570 --> 00:11:35.320
designs and also makes sure
that every single person

00:11:35.320 --> 00:11:36.740
in the company
goes through them.

00:11:39.980 --> 00:11:42.730
Our employees also
have to go through code

00:11:42.730 --> 00:11:45.730
of conduct training,
and that training

00:11:45.730 --> 00:11:50.110
explains the best practices
and expected behavior

00:11:50.110 --> 00:11:51.520
for data protection.

00:11:54.240 --> 00:11:59.510
So what do we do specifically,
besides the contracts, when

00:11:59.510 --> 00:12:02.990
we assist the data
controllers, you the customers,

00:12:02.990 --> 00:12:05.870
in GDPR compliance?

00:12:05.870 --> 00:12:08.360
As I mentioned,
data subject rights

00:12:08.360 --> 00:12:12.200
are incredibly important
under the GDPR.

00:12:12.200 --> 00:12:15.980
And under the GDPR,
you have to prepare

00:12:15.980 --> 00:12:18.320
for data subject requests to--

00:12:18.320 --> 00:12:23.990
they will ask to access,
rectify, and restrict

00:12:23.990 --> 00:12:25.580
their data processing.

00:12:25.580 --> 00:12:28.940
And we do have built-in
functionality and products

00:12:28.940 --> 00:12:33.130
where these requests
can be easily met.

00:12:33.130 --> 00:12:36.310
We also have a
dedicated team who

00:12:36.310 --> 00:12:39.565
can respond in case
you have any data

00:12:39.565 --> 00:12:40.690
protection-related enquiry.

00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:49.600
We had turned on
incident notifications

00:12:49.600 --> 00:12:53.950
for several years, so
it's nothing new to us.

00:12:53.950 --> 00:12:56.770
And we continue to
promptly notify them

00:12:56.770 --> 00:12:59.380
when we become aware
of an incident.

00:12:59.380 --> 00:13:01.870
And this is very
important under the GDPR,

00:13:01.870 --> 00:13:07.850
because the GDPR has really
strict incident notification

00:13:07.850 --> 00:13:08.890
timelines.

00:13:08.890 --> 00:13:11.960
For data controller,
for the customer,

00:13:11.960 --> 00:13:18.040
it's 72 hours after you
became aware of an incident.

00:13:18.040 --> 00:13:20.380
And within this
72 hours, you have

00:13:20.380 --> 00:13:24.370
to notify the
supervisory authorities.

00:13:24.370 --> 00:13:27.470
Us data processors,
on the other hand,

00:13:27.470 --> 00:13:32.130
we have to notify
you, the controller,

00:13:32.130 --> 00:13:34.340
without undue delay.

00:13:34.340 --> 00:13:36.900
And again, you can find
all these terms in our data

00:13:36.900 --> 00:13:38.700
processing amendments.

00:13:38.700 --> 00:13:41.730
And as I mentioned,
I really want

00:13:41.730 --> 00:13:45.750
to call your attention to
our GDPR Resource Center,

00:13:45.750 --> 00:13:51.840
which you can very easily
find by Googling GDPR, Google,

00:13:51.840 --> 00:13:53.130
and Resource Center.

00:13:53.130 --> 00:13:57.660
And on the top banner, as I'm
showing you on this slide,

00:13:57.660 --> 00:14:01.810
you can see where you can click
and opt in to the new terms.

00:14:01.810 --> 00:14:03.780
And please do so to
make sure that you

00:14:03.780 --> 00:14:08.670
have an agreement which
reflects the GDPR changes.

00:14:08.670 --> 00:14:11.730
On this website, you can
also find more details

00:14:11.730 --> 00:14:13.510
about the things
I described today,

00:14:13.510 --> 00:14:16.410
including some of
the product features

00:14:16.410 --> 00:14:20.430
I'll describe at the end.

00:14:20.430 --> 00:14:26.190
So number two, use
of sub-processors.

00:14:26.190 --> 00:14:31.425
Today's business environment
is a little more complex

00:14:31.425 --> 00:14:34.150
as having just one
data controller

00:14:34.150 --> 00:14:36.390
and one data processor.

00:14:36.390 --> 00:14:40.860
And we, Google,
as well, in cases,

00:14:40.860 --> 00:14:47.750
rely on third-party vendors
processing customer data.

00:14:47.750 --> 00:14:53.060
And it's very important that
these third party vendors

00:14:53.060 --> 00:14:59.390
go through very, very rigorous
security and selection process.

00:14:59.390 --> 00:15:05.360
And even the onboarding
process is very, very rigorous.

00:15:05.360 --> 00:15:09.620
And for example, they have
to go through strict security

00:15:09.620 --> 00:15:11.000
testing.

00:15:11.000 --> 00:15:15.170
We are also transparent about
the sub-processor we use.

00:15:15.170 --> 00:15:17.250
You can find them
on our website,

00:15:17.250 --> 00:15:21.710
and also, there is a link in
the data processing agreement

00:15:21.710 --> 00:15:24.450
where you can review
the list of them.

00:15:29.190 --> 00:15:31.525
Data subject rights
is a third point.

00:15:31.525 --> 00:15:34.990
And I decided to use two
specific data subject

00:15:34.990 --> 00:15:38.680
rights to demonstrate
how we in G Suite

00:15:38.680 --> 00:15:43.120
can support in case customers
come to you with data subject

00:15:43.120 --> 00:15:45.730
requests.

00:15:45.730 --> 00:15:47.550
The first one is data return.

00:15:47.550 --> 00:15:52.450
So I mentioned at the beginning
that there are new data subject

00:15:52.450 --> 00:15:54.600
rights under the GDPR.

00:15:54.600 --> 00:16:01.630
And one was one of them is
right to data portability.

00:16:01.630 --> 00:16:03.030
What does it mean?

00:16:03.030 --> 00:16:07.540
Data subjects, these have
directives as their data

00:16:07.540 --> 00:16:09.370
even before they GDPR.

00:16:09.370 --> 00:16:13.170
But the GDPR takes
it even further.

00:16:13.170 --> 00:16:17.970
It requires that
the data controllers

00:16:17.970 --> 00:16:22.110
have to make this data
available to customers

00:16:22.110 --> 00:16:26.460
in a machine-readable structure
in a commonly-used format.

00:16:26.460 --> 00:16:30.390
They should be able to
export it and transmit

00:16:30.390 --> 00:16:34.080
to another controller
if they wish,

00:16:34.080 --> 00:16:37.980
without any further hindrance
from the controller.

00:16:37.980 --> 00:16:42.380
Now, Google had that
feature even before the GDPR

00:16:42.380 --> 00:16:46.920
that made it available
both for our customers

00:16:46.920 --> 00:16:52.800
to retrieve their data, export
it, and take it to somewhere.

00:16:52.800 --> 00:16:56.010
It's called Google Takeout.

00:16:56.010 --> 00:16:59.040
And also in G Suite,
the administrators

00:16:59.040 --> 00:17:04.230
were able to export customer
data via the functionality of G

00:17:04.230 --> 00:17:06.490
Suite.

00:17:06.490 --> 00:17:10.010
But for GDPR, we
took it even further,

00:17:10.010 --> 00:17:14.079
and we recently,
before the GDPR,

00:17:14.079 --> 00:17:18.290
launched an enhanced
data export feature

00:17:18.290 --> 00:17:24.790
that enables you to download
data for all of your domain.

00:17:24.790 --> 00:17:27.280
And we made sure that
you can do it securely,

00:17:27.280 --> 00:17:32.300
so we added the additional
security controls in there.

00:17:32.300 --> 00:17:36.440
As an example, only super admins
who have been with the company

00:17:36.440 --> 00:17:38.120
for 30 days can do that.

00:17:38.120 --> 00:17:42.280
They have to use
additional authentication,

00:17:42.280 --> 00:17:44.570
multi-factor authentication.

00:17:44.570 --> 00:17:46.880
Whenever there is
multiple super admins,

00:17:46.880 --> 00:17:51.890
we promptly notify them if
such export was initiated.

00:17:51.890 --> 00:17:54.680
And also, you can
only do this if you

00:17:54.680 --> 00:18:02.060
have less than 1,000 employees.

00:18:02.060 --> 00:18:05.630
Otherwise, we have to go
through the Google Support Team,

00:18:05.630 --> 00:18:09.020
and they can temporarily
enable the feature, again,

00:18:09.020 --> 00:18:14.630
to make sure that the data
export happens securely.

00:18:14.630 --> 00:18:16.090
The next one is data deletion.

00:18:16.090 --> 00:18:20.770
Data deletion is not
a new right in GDPR,

00:18:20.770 --> 00:18:23.386
but it is still very important.

00:18:23.386 --> 00:18:24.760
Because we want
to make sure that

00:18:24.760 --> 00:18:30.070
we only store data until
its necessary for business

00:18:30.070 --> 00:18:32.030
purposes.

00:18:32.030 --> 00:18:34.360
So we always had
built-in functionality

00:18:34.360 --> 00:18:37.660
in G Suite and all
of its products

00:18:37.660 --> 00:18:45.560
that made it possible for you
to delete customer data anytime.

00:18:45.560 --> 00:18:50.320
And we also, as we mentioned
also in our data processing

00:18:50.320 --> 00:18:55.620
amendment and also in a
recent public statement,

00:18:55.620 --> 00:18:59.410
we make sure that relevant
customer data, in case we

00:18:59.410 --> 00:19:02.030
receive a deletion
request from you,

00:19:02.030 --> 00:19:08.210
it will be deleted from
our system within 180 days,

00:19:08.210 --> 00:19:12.670
unless, of course, there are
some retention obligations

00:19:12.670 --> 00:19:13.540
that apply.

00:19:13.540 --> 00:19:19.510
And this can be a EU member
state's law requirement

00:19:19.510 --> 00:19:27.770
or a litigation hoard or tax
authorize this sometimes also,

00:19:27.770 --> 00:19:33.530
wants us to retain data
longer, as an example.

00:19:33.530 --> 00:19:34.450
And now, the fun.

00:19:34.450 --> 00:19:39.400
So probably when you heard first
about the GDPR in the news,

00:19:39.400 --> 00:19:42.800
you read about the
incredible fines.

00:19:42.800 --> 00:19:47.890
The fines under the GDPR
are incredibly high.

00:19:47.890 --> 00:19:50.600
No other data
protection legislation

00:19:50.600 --> 00:19:56.800
could impose such incredible
fines as the GDPR,

00:19:56.800 --> 00:20:00.640
and whenever you need
attention from more executives

00:20:00.640 --> 00:20:05.910
at your company, I recommend
you to bring up the fines.

00:20:05.910 --> 00:20:09.050
That always gets
their attention.

00:20:09.050 --> 00:20:12.100
And the fine, of course,
can be, as maximum fine,

00:20:12.100 --> 00:20:17.760
20 million euros, or 4%
of the annual turnover.

00:20:17.760 --> 00:20:23.610
The GDPR gives example, what
are the scenarios and what

00:20:23.610 --> 00:20:27.180
are the type of infringements
these maximum fines

00:20:27.180 --> 00:20:28.320
can be imposed?

00:20:28.320 --> 00:20:32.520
And these are related to the
data protection principles

00:20:32.520 --> 00:20:34.480
I mentioned at the
beginning, data subject

00:20:34.480 --> 00:20:39.570
rights or international
data transfer mechanisms.

00:20:39.570 --> 00:20:42.930
And the GDPR also
gives a detailed list

00:20:42.930 --> 00:20:45.360
of what are the
deciding factors,

00:20:45.360 --> 00:20:47.280
the data protection
authorities that

00:20:47.280 --> 00:20:51.540
can impose these
fines are looking to,

00:20:51.540 --> 00:20:56.130
and things that can be
relevant, the type of data

00:20:56.130 --> 00:20:59.590
that was [INAUDIBLE]
for the infringement.

00:20:59.590 --> 00:21:03.360
These are sensitive
persona data, for example.

00:21:03.360 --> 00:21:07.530
It also depends
on your behavior.

00:21:07.530 --> 00:21:11.370
Was the infringement the
result of a negligent

00:21:11.370 --> 00:21:14.280
or an intentional behavior?

00:21:14.280 --> 00:21:17.820
Did you have any similar
relevant previous

00:21:17.820 --> 00:21:19.080
infringements?

00:21:19.080 --> 00:21:25.500
How much did you do to
mitigate the damage that

00:21:25.500 --> 00:21:29.530
resulted from the infringement?

00:21:29.530 --> 00:21:32.550
How much did you cooperate
with the data protection

00:21:32.550 --> 00:21:33.600
authorities?

00:21:33.600 --> 00:21:37.230
So these are all deciding
factors, and there is more

00:21:37.230 --> 00:21:40.285
in the GDPR in Article 83.

00:21:44.530 --> 00:21:48.640
And finally, as a
fifth point, I wanted

00:21:48.640 --> 00:21:53.230
to talk about the
GDPR and security,

00:21:53.230 --> 00:21:55.690
because GDPR is a
data protection law,

00:21:55.690 --> 00:22:02.370
so it has privacy and
security provisions as well.

00:22:02.370 --> 00:22:07.180
Such as other things,
the GDPR has provisions

00:22:07.180 --> 00:22:11.990
and describes responsibilities
both for the data controller

00:22:11.990 --> 00:22:14.680
and the data processor.

00:22:14.680 --> 00:22:20.080
So you and the customer and
us are both responsible.

00:22:20.080 --> 00:22:23.470
And security is really
at the core of the GDPR.

00:22:23.470 --> 00:22:29.050
The GDPR doesn't give you an
exhaustive list of product

00:22:29.050 --> 00:22:34.630
features that you must have
in order to be GDPR compliant,

00:22:34.630 --> 00:22:40.630
and, well, it would be
very difficult because

00:22:40.630 --> 00:22:41.980
of the changing technology.

00:22:41.980 --> 00:22:48.280
But it does describe
that you have to--

00:22:48.280 --> 00:22:50.710
both the controller and the
processor have to perform

00:22:50.710 --> 00:22:58.090
a risk assessment based on
the recent state-of-the-art

00:22:58.090 --> 00:23:01.755
industry-accepted best
practices in security.

00:23:01.755 --> 00:23:04.300
And actually, the
GDPR does bring up

00:23:04.300 --> 00:23:08.440
two examples, [INAUDIBLE]
and encryptions

00:23:08.440 --> 00:23:11.500
as some of these best examples.

00:23:11.500 --> 00:23:14.110
But really, the
point is that you

00:23:14.110 --> 00:23:17.500
have to be able to
protect your user

00:23:17.500 --> 00:23:23.080
data from unlawful access,
unauthorized access,

00:23:23.080 --> 00:23:26.980
and unlawful processing.

00:23:26.980 --> 00:23:30.070
So what are some
of these examples

00:23:30.070 --> 00:23:36.420
we have in G Suite that you can
leverage in GDPR compliance?

00:23:36.420 --> 00:23:39.720
I want to talk
about these features

00:23:39.720 --> 00:23:43.740
in the context of access
monitoring and access

00:23:43.740 --> 00:23:46.770
control, because these are very
important for your security

00:23:46.770 --> 00:23:47.710
best practices.

00:23:52.290 --> 00:23:55.890
And again, I've highlighted
the encryption here,

00:23:55.890 --> 00:24:03.770
because the GDPR does
highlight encryption as one

00:24:03.770 --> 00:24:06.530
of the examples of
security best practices.

00:24:09.640 --> 00:24:14.520
The first one is
two-step verification.

00:24:14.520 --> 00:24:18.320
Why do companies need
two-step verification?

00:24:18.320 --> 00:24:22.900
Well, we know that passwords
can be easily compromised.

00:24:22.900 --> 00:24:24.660
They can be figured out.

00:24:24.660 --> 00:24:27.180
They can be stolen.

00:24:27.180 --> 00:24:31.770
So it's very important
that, especially

00:24:31.770 --> 00:24:34.170
in terms of enterprise
corporate data,

00:24:34.170 --> 00:24:38.490
which is more
vulnerable in terms

00:24:38.490 --> 00:24:44.960
of security vulnerability--

00:24:44.960 --> 00:24:49.310
the two-step verification
gives you an additional layer

00:24:49.310 --> 00:24:51.720
when it asks users
when they sign in

00:24:51.720 --> 00:24:54.990
for additional proof
of identification.

00:24:54.990 --> 00:24:59.460
And one good example
is a security key,

00:24:59.460 --> 00:25:04.080
a physical security
key that you can

00:25:04.080 --> 00:25:09.600
plug into your laptop,
monitor, or desktop.

00:25:09.600 --> 00:25:12.970
So this is definitely
highly recommended.

00:25:12.970 --> 00:25:14.640
We use this.

00:25:14.640 --> 00:25:19.550
All employees of Google
are required to use it

00:25:19.550 --> 00:25:22.520
as a security best practice.

00:25:22.520 --> 00:25:26.705
Suspicious login monitoring--
so suspicious logging

00:25:26.705 --> 00:25:30.500
and monitoring uses
Google's robust machine

00:25:30.500 --> 00:25:35.090
learning capabilities to
detect suspicious logins.

00:25:35.090 --> 00:25:40.730
And in order to use
this, administrators

00:25:40.730 --> 00:25:43.550
can sign up for email alerts.

00:25:43.550 --> 00:25:50.670
And we look at possibly
suspicious behavior examples.

00:25:50.670 --> 00:25:54.800
For example, if
someone tries to log in

00:25:54.800 --> 00:25:58.970
to your corporate account
from an unusual location

00:25:58.970 --> 00:26:04.820
or from a new unknown
device, first the process

00:26:04.820 --> 00:26:11.490
is, in most cases, we send a
login challenge to the user.

00:26:11.490 --> 00:26:16.070
And we typically ask them--

00:26:16.070 --> 00:26:20.630
we send them a passcode
that they have to enter.

00:26:20.630 --> 00:26:23.980
We send them a passcode in email
or either cell phone, whichever

00:26:23.980 --> 00:26:26.810
was their selected method.

00:26:26.810 --> 00:26:31.490
And if they're unable to solve
this challenge or avoid it,

00:26:31.490 --> 00:26:37.400
then this is when we send
a very clear notification

00:26:37.400 --> 00:26:43.260
to the administrator so
that it can take an action--

00:26:46.770 --> 00:26:48.000
or he can take action.

00:26:53.360 --> 00:26:55.560
Mobile Device Management, MDM--

00:26:55.560 --> 00:27:01.380
so we recently
launched a feature

00:27:01.380 --> 00:27:09.390
that we announced the next
terms of MDM, mobile device

00:27:09.390 --> 00:27:15.470
management, as part of or
Cloud Identity SSO catalogue.

00:27:15.470 --> 00:27:20.120
And this is great,
because now, basic MDM

00:27:20.120 --> 00:27:25.145
comes by default on [INAUDIBLE]
corporate mobile devices.

00:27:28.180 --> 00:27:30.220
And that means that
employees don't

00:27:30.220 --> 00:27:35.300
have to download additional
profiles in order to use this,

00:27:35.300 --> 00:27:39.525
but it comes by default both
on Android and iOS devices.

00:27:42.380 --> 00:27:45.370
It helps the
administrator, because it

00:27:45.370 --> 00:27:52.820
gives a single dashboard view
where the administrator can

00:27:52.820 --> 00:27:56.255
see all the devices that
can access corporate data.

00:27:59.190 --> 00:28:06.500
It makes it very easy to enforce
passcodes or remove content

00:28:06.500 --> 00:28:11.160
or wipe devices remotely in
case they get compromised.

00:28:11.160 --> 00:28:12.910
And again, the great
thing about this

00:28:12.910 --> 00:28:18.890
is that no user
intervention needed,

00:28:18.890 --> 00:28:22.140
and this is very helpful
in today's world.

00:28:22.140 --> 00:28:25.520
Especially when
corporations have

00:28:25.520 --> 00:28:30.160
to deal with the [INAUDIBLE]
of the devices, for example,

00:28:30.160 --> 00:28:33.930
the solution works really well.

00:28:33.930 --> 00:28:37.700
And of course, as I mentioned,
it comes with the basic MDM

00:28:37.700 --> 00:28:39.770
feature by default,
but you can always

00:28:39.770 --> 00:28:41.600
select more advanced settings.

00:28:44.680 --> 00:28:46.790
Data Loss Prevention, or DLP--

00:28:50.040 --> 00:28:53.970
DLP uses Optical Character
Recognition, or OCR,

00:28:53.970 --> 00:28:58.080
technologies to scan
content in Gmail,

00:28:58.080 --> 00:29:01.440
including its
attachments, files,

00:29:01.440 --> 00:29:04.750
and in Drive and Team Drives.

00:29:04.750 --> 00:29:09.130
And it's looking for
possibly sensitive content.

00:29:09.130 --> 00:29:11.940
We privacy professionals
are aware of what

00:29:11.940 --> 00:29:15.600
can be potentially
sensitive data

00:29:15.600 --> 00:29:18.540
both globally and
country-specifically.

00:29:18.540 --> 00:29:22.480
As an example, we know that
credit card information

00:29:22.480 --> 00:29:28.050
and bank account
information is sensitive,

00:29:28.050 --> 00:29:30.990
so we have these, by
default, turned on.

00:29:30.990 --> 00:29:35.070
And you can very easily turn
these predefined detectors

00:29:35.070 --> 00:29:38.250
on and off, depending on which
countries you operate in,

00:29:38.250 --> 00:29:40.500
for example.

00:29:40.500 --> 00:29:43.020
You also define your
own custom rules,

00:29:43.020 --> 00:29:45.030
because you are the one
who knows your business.

00:29:45.030 --> 00:29:50.310
You are the one who knows what's
sensitive data for you in terms

00:29:50.310 --> 00:29:56.010
of intellectual property
and sensitive project codes.

00:29:56.010 --> 00:29:59.790
And you can find these
rules by keywords

00:29:59.790 --> 00:30:02.570
or using regular expressions.

00:30:02.570 --> 00:30:05.490
And if you observe
that you receive

00:30:05.490 --> 00:30:09.720
too many false positives, you
can always adjust these content

00:30:09.720 --> 00:30:10.410
thresholds.

00:30:14.030 --> 00:30:16.910
Information rights management
is a next good example.

00:30:19.820 --> 00:30:26.600
We do have it available both
for admins within the admin

00:30:26.600 --> 00:30:28.460
console and users.

00:30:28.460 --> 00:30:33.020
And what it means is that the
admins can limit file access

00:30:33.020 --> 00:30:34.710
privileges.

00:30:34.710 --> 00:30:36.890
For example, you
may want to restrict

00:30:36.890 --> 00:30:40.160
certain sensitive files and
make sure that they can only

00:30:40.160 --> 00:30:44.030
be shared within the domain.

00:30:44.030 --> 00:30:47.010
And it gives you
greater control,

00:30:47.010 --> 00:30:51.395
so you can define different
rules for teams, for example.

00:30:55.055 --> 00:30:58.310
The sales team, as
an example, are not

00:30:58.310 --> 00:31:04.810
allowed to share certain type of
files with an external domain.

00:31:04.810 --> 00:31:09.670
And it also adds IRM controls,
additional information rights

00:31:09.670 --> 00:31:15.980
management controls both
for the admin and the user.

00:31:15.980 --> 00:31:18.190
And here, I am
showing how it looks

00:31:18.190 --> 00:31:20.350
from the user perspective.

00:31:20.350 --> 00:31:23.350
So first of all,
by default, it's

00:31:23.350 --> 00:31:25.420
only shared by the
user who created it.

00:31:25.420 --> 00:31:29.320
And they have to take additional
actions, additional clicks

00:31:29.320 --> 00:31:32.590
if they want to
share it with others.

00:31:32.590 --> 00:31:40.060
And also, again, both
administrators and user are--

00:31:40.060 --> 00:31:42.310
can very easily, by
checking checkboxes,

00:31:42.310 --> 00:31:46.600
can disable downloading,
printing, and copying files.

00:31:50.600 --> 00:31:56.830
And finally, I wanted to talk
about enhanced email security.

00:31:59.820 --> 00:32:03.762
Gmail now offers S/MIME.

00:32:03.762 --> 00:32:08.330
And S/MIME stands for
Secure/Multipurpose Internet

00:32:08.330 --> 00:32:09.840
Mail Extension.

00:32:09.840 --> 00:32:13.100
And this is why we
prefer to call S/MIME.

00:32:13.100 --> 00:32:19.380
And really, secure is
an important word here.

00:32:19.380 --> 00:32:22.850
So this is now the
industry standard way

00:32:22.850 --> 00:32:24.300
of encrypting e-mails.

00:32:24.300 --> 00:32:32.020
And it requires e-mails
to be signed and encrypted

00:32:32.020 --> 00:32:35.140
when then they send it out.

00:32:35.140 --> 00:32:41.970
And it's also important that you
can bring your own certificates

00:32:41.970 --> 00:32:44.440
and use that with S/MIME.

00:32:44.440 --> 00:32:51.070
But Gmail has additional
security features,

00:32:51.070 --> 00:32:55.440
such as TLS and DKIM warnings.

00:32:55.440 --> 00:32:57.750
And I show an
example here that we

00:32:57.750 --> 00:33:03.150
try to give very, very
clear visual signals in case

00:33:03.150 --> 00:33:11.470
we suspect that a user
receives a message

00:33:11.470 --> 00:33:16.340
from an unauthenticated source.

00:33:16.340 --> 00:33:22.580
And also, we try to give very
clear visual signals if we see

00:33:22.580 --> 00:33:25.920
that a message was unencrypted.

00:33:31.560 --> 00:33:34.680
So this was the last
security feature

00:33:34.680 --> 00:33:35.940
I wanted to talk about today.

00:33:35.940 --> 00:33:38.700
But of course, there are more.

00:33:38.700 --> 00:33:41.550
And you'll find some
of these features

00:33:41.550 --> 00:33:49.240
both for G Suite and GCP at
our GDPR Resource Center.

00:33:52.610 --> 00:33:59.090
So please stay tuned for Q&amp;A.
We'll be back in less than a

00:33:59.090 --> 00:33:59.590
minute.

00:36:33.505 --> 00:36:34.005
Hi.

00:36:34.005 --> 00:36:35.420
Welcome back.

00:36:35.420 --> 00:36:39.830
We received some questions
that I would like to answer.

00:36:39.830 --> 00:36:43.370
The first question is
an interesting one.

00:36:43.370 --> 00:36:48.440
Does the GDPR require data
to be stored in the EU, data

00:36:48.440 --> 00:36:49.340
localization?

00:36:49.340 --> 00:36:53.870
So one great thing about
the GDPR, that it does not

00:36:53.870 --> 00:36:57.020
require data localization.

00:36:57.020 --> 00:37:00.410
It does enhance the
data transfer mechanism,

00:37:00.410 --> 00:37:05.570
so it does require that
there is an adequate level

00:37:05.570 --> 00:37:08.330
of data protection
from data that's

00:37:08.330 --> 00:37:11.150
flowing outside of the EU.

00:37:11.150 --> 00:37:14.930
And the GDPR lists
certain mechanisms

00:37:14.930 --> 00:37:18.500
how this can be achieved
or recognized by DPAs.

00:37:18.500 --> 00:37:24.280
And some of these are the
privacy shared, for example,

00:37:24.280 --> 00:37:28.820
or [? model ?] contract
clauses that Google uses.

00:37:28.820 --> 00:37:33.060
And GDPR mentions other
mechanisms as well,

00:37:33.060 --> 00:37:36.710
for example, binding
corporate rules,

00:37:36.710 --> 00:37:42.160
and you can have
certification as well.

00:37:42.160 --> 00:37:43.430
But this is very important.

00:37:43.430 --> 00:37:47.230
So as the EU, for
the European Union,

00:37:47.230 --> 00:37:53.680
actually, it's aimed to make
sure that the data flows freely

00:37:53.680 --> 00:37:59.210
within the EU, so there are no
data localization requirements.

00:37:59.210 --> 00:38:03.220
The second question is, will
all these security features

00:38:03.220 --> 00:38:04.885
be used only for EU citizens?

00:38:07.530 --> 00:38:08.935
I've got this question before.

00:38:08.935 --> 00:38:11.730
It's a very good question.

00:38:11.730 --> 00:38:16.520
And as someone who worked
on these privacy programs,

00:38:16.520 --> 00:38:19.630
we really do not
differentiate between EU

00:38:19.630 --> 00:38:23.950
citizens and other
citizens when we have

00:38:23.950 --> 00:38:25.870
been preparing for the GDPR.

00:38:25.870 --> 00:38:29.380
So we make these features
available to everyone,

00:38:29.380 --> 00:38:32.890
because we believe these
are-- the GDPR has great data

00:38:32.890 --> 00:38:34.450
protection practices.

00:38:34.450 --> 00:38:38.050
We believe that data subject
rights are important.

00:38:38.050 --> 00:38:40.450
We agree with all the data
protection principles.

00:38:40.450 --> 00:38:44.110
So we really want to make
sure that the good things that

00:38:44.110 --> 00:38:47.490
came with GDPR are
available for everyone.

00:38:52.870 --> 00:38:55.580
The third question is about
the GDPR Resource Center,

00:38:55.580 --> 00:39:01.320
and I have been talking about
the Resource Center a lot.

00:39:01.320 --> 00:39:06.130
So again, it's very easy to find
just by Googling GDPR, Google,

00:39:06.130 --> 00:39:07.870
and Resource Center.

00:39:07.870 --> 00:39:14.205
But we will provide a link
in the platform on the Q&amp;A

00:39:14.205 --> 00:39:14.705
session.

00:39:19.880 --> 00:39:25.020
Someone was asking how can
they find out more about DLP.

00:39:25.020 --> 00:39:28.870
We have white paper,
help center articles.

00:39:28.870 --> 00:39:33.770
And those are good
examples, again.

00:39:33.770 --> 00:39:38.260
And if I remember correctly,
you can actually find these.

00:39:38.260 --> 00:39:41.110
Again, just to go to the
GDPR Resource Center.

00:39:41.110 --> 00:39:45.400
And when we talk about
product features that

00:39:45.400 --> 00:39:49.120
can be helpful with the
GDPR, they are linked there.

00:39:49.120 --> 00:39:55.390
But again, you can also
just Google DLP or Data Loss

00:39:55.390 --> 00:39:58.020
Prevention white paper.

00:39:58.020 --> 00:40:02.980
And both the actual white paper
and other help center articles

00:40:02.980 --> 00:40:04.960
will pop up.

00:40:04.960 --> 00:40:09.340
And same goes for GCP.

00:40:09.340 --> 00:40:15.150
You can learn about DLP
API for GCP as well.

00:40:21.740 --> 00:40:25.550
OK, so the last
question is, is there

00:40:25.550 --> 00:40:31.540
a continuity between GDPR
compliance in G Suite and GCP?

00:40:31.540 --> 00:40:34.910
What are the counterparts in
the larger Cloud offering?

00:40:34.910 --> 00:40:39.980
Which features you presented
are also available in GCP?

00:40:39.980 --> 00:40:43.200
It's a great question.

00:40:43.200 --> 00:40:47.510
Yes, there is a continuity
between GDPR compliance

00:40:47.510 --> 00:40:49.400
within G Suite and GCP.

00:40:49.400 --> 00:40:52.220
I am in G Suite, but
we work very closely

00:40:52.220 --> 00:40:54.080
with the GCP teams.

00:40:54.080 --> 00:40:58.850
And they have similar
features-- not exactly the same,

00:40:58.850 --> 00:41:00.920
because the product
offering is different.

00:41:00.920 --> 00:41:05.640
But they also have,
as an example,

00:41:05.640 --> 00:41:12.080
a two-step verification for
the DLP, Data Loss Prevention.

00:41:12.080 --> 00:41:15.150
They have DLP APIs.

00:41:15.150 --> 00:41:20.490
They also offer various
products for access monitoring

00:41:20.490 --> 00:41:21.950
and control.

00:41:21.950 --> 00:41:25.530
Some of them are
product-specific.

00:41:25.530 --> 00:41:27.920
For example, they have
Stackdriver monitoring

00:41:27.920 --> 00:41:35.470
and logging, Cloud security
scanners, I believe,

00:41:35.470 --> 00:41:40.810
[INAUDIBLE],, and also
Security Command Center.

00:41:40.810 --> 00:41:44.720
So I actually didn't go through
all the security features,

00:41:44.720 --> 00:41:47.250
because there are many.

00:41:47.250 --> 00:41:53.360
But we have Google Security
Center and Google Command

00:41:53.360 --> 00:41:57.410
Center for G Suite
and GCP, which

00:41:57.410 --> 00:42:01.490
is very helpful for
the administrators,

00:42:01.490 --> 00:42:10.160
because it can give them an
overall view of the G Suite

00:42:10.160 --> 00:42:17.470
products and
security positioning.

00:42:17.470 --> 00:42:19.750
So those are very helpful.

00:42:19.750 --> 00:42:25.090
And in case I don't
remember all of them,

00:42:25.090 --> 00:42:29.230
again, these product
features and more are

00:42:29.230 --> 00:42:33.440
available in the
GDPR Resource Center.

00:42:38.900 --> 00:42:44.525
So I don't know if we
have more questions, but--

00:42:47.350 --> 00:42:48.520
nope?

00:42:48.520 --> 00:42:50.240
So we don't have more questions.

00:42:50.240 --> 00:42:55.030
So again, I wanted to tell you
that we do these live webinars

00:42:55.030 --> 00:42:56.780
every Tuesday.

00:42:56.780 --> 00:43:01.310
You can check it on the
Cloud on our website.

00:43:01.310 --> 00:43:06.350
And we have a nice
session coming up.

00:43:06.350 --> 00:43:09.380
And stay tuned for
it-- this is a CE Chat,

00:43:09.380 --> 00:43:11.940
Observability with OpenCensus.

00:43:11.940 --> 00:43:16.520
And thank you so much
for joining in today.

